- op: create  # bf11
  at: '2026-05-04T05:28:48Z'  # bf11
  by: agent  # bf11
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bf11
  clock: 1  # bf11
  nonce: bf11  # bf11
  data:  # bf11
    kind: invariant  # bf11
    title: "Linker simple-name fallback edges must carry confidence <= 0.5 + disambiguation_fallback flag"  # bf11
    status: violated  # bf11
    priority: 2  # bf11
    parent:  # bf11
    tags:  # bf11
    - analysis_quality  # bf11
    isbefore: []  # bf11
    duplicate_of: []  # bf11
    not_duplicate_of: []  # bf11
    pr_ref:  # bf11
    description: "**Source:** promoted from WI-pudil (UAT DQ-06, status: wont_do, see closing\nnote linking here). Decision made via 2026-05-04 interview after\n`hg-uat-v4.0.0` directed-validation campaign surfaced new instances.\n\n**Statement (also in fields.statement):** Linker-emitted edges whose target\nis resolved via simple-name fallback — i.e. without a fully-qualified\nmodule / namespace / kind disambiguator — must carry **both**:\n\n1. **`confidence ≤ 0.5`** on the edge.\n2. **A `disambiguation_fallback=true` provenance flag** (or equivalent\n   meta key, to be standardized) so downstream consumers can filter\n   the fallback population from the precision-resolved population.\n\nThis applies to every edge type that participates in name resolution:\n`extends`, `implements`, `calls`, `dispatches_to`, `references`,\n`module_exports`, and any future edge type whose producer falls back to\nshort-name match when full resolution is unavailable.\n\n## Why this is an invariant, not a per-linker task\n\n### The implicit umbrella that already exists\n\nFor `extends` / `implements` edges, the project already has parallel\nproperty tests across **at least 5 languages** (Python, JS/TS, Ruby,\nKotlin, Java) — listed under the `regression_tests` field of the\ndeleted INV-jodak (which was a template-cruft tombstone, not a\ndeliberate rejection of the umbrella; see WI-pudil close-note for the\nforensic walk):\n\n- `test_extends_prefers_imported_class_over_name_collision` (×5 langs)\n- `test_extends_same_file_class_preferred_over_other_file` (×5 langs)\n- `test_extends_deterministic_fallback_when_ambiguous` (×5 langs)\n- `test_implements_prefers_imported_interface_over_collision` (Java, JS/TS, Kotlin)\n- `test_extends_import_matches_full_fqn_path` (Java)\n\nThat's 18+ tests enforcing the *narrow* version of this invariant\n(extends/implements only) without it ever being written down as one.\nThis INV writes the implicit umbrella down and **extends it to the\nedge-type families that don't yet honor it**.\n\n### Existing low-confidence convention\n\n`notebookjournal_04062026_1634.md` (lab notebook) records the existing\ninformal convention as already in use:\n> \"specific to that 1 — almost always a name collision or cross-package\n> short-name collision, **conf 0.30 (test-downweighted)**, separated\"\n\nSo the `confidence ≤ 0.5` clause **formalizes a practice already in\nuse**, not a new constraint.\n\n### Recurrence count\n\n≈11 done WI items address this exact pattern across linkers / languages,\nplus the following recent uncaught instances:\n\n- **Apr 2026 (Go cross-package `Notifier` struct collision)** —\n  `bf4oxh91w_investigation_threads_04072026_1702.md` lab notebook entry,\n  fixed in commit `d52a1ee2b`. Eighteen packages each declared\n  `type Notifier struct`; `struct_syms[\"Notifier\"]` was first-wins by\n  short-name keying, merging method sets across packages. Caught only\n  because the agent kept digging past an unrelated arity fix.\n- **May 2026 (BUG-03 / WI-zozuz, Rust implements-edge)** — see\n  description. Two layers: kind disambiguation within Rust (`impl Module\n  for LayerNorm` resolves to `Module` *struct* not *trait*); cross-\n  language amplification (Python `nn.Module` subclasses \"implementing\"\n  the Rust `Module` trait via unqualified-name match).\n- **May 2026 (kafka `Transformer<T>` collision)** — see WI-lisov\n  discuss-note (round 08 / DQ-14). `Transformer<T>` class in oauthbearer\n  code treated by `kafka_streams_dispatch` as the `Transformer`\n  interface; confidence 0.5 already, but no provenance flag, so\n  downstream consumers can't reliably filter.\n\nThe recurrence rate is high enough — and the pattern is shaped enough —\nthat the case-by-case treatment is empirically not converging.\n\n## Concrete known violations as of filing\n\n| Surface | Linker / construct | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust `implements`-edge (kind disambiguation + cross-language gating) | `WI-tulid` linker (the original implements-edge linker) | **WI-zozuz** (UAT BUG-03) |\n| JVM `dispatches_to` from `kafka_streams_dispatch` (`Transformer<T>` simple-name match) | `kafka_streams_dispatch.py` | WI-lisov discuss-note (round 08 / DQ-14) |\n| Go `interface_dispatch` (residual cases the WI-jopar receiver-type guard doesn't cover — `r.Close()` / `req.Header.Set(...)` resolving to project-internal `Alerts.Close`/`Set` per UAT round 02 / DQ-2) | `interface_dispatch` Go linker | WI-likab discuss-note (UAT round 02) |\n\nThese are the concrete `violated` instances that fixing constitutes\nenforcement evidence.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nThe invariant moves from `violated` → `pending_validation` when:\n\n1. Every linker that emits any of the listed edge types ships parallel\n   property tests of the form `test_<edge_type>_prefers_imported_*`,\n   `test_<edge_type>_same_file_preferred_over_other_file`,\n   `test_<edge_type>_deterministic_fallback_when_ambiguous`.\n2. A property test in `tests/` AST-walks every linker module and asserts:\n   any code path that emits an edge whose target was resolved by simple-\n   name fallback either (a) sets `confidence ≤ 0.5` and `meta[\"disambiguation_fallback\"] = True`, OR (b) sets a sentinel that\n   the property test recognizes as \"not a fallback case.\" A drift\n   linter (cf. `scripts/check-edge-type-drift`) at pre-commit fails on\n   violations.\n3. The three concrete instances above ship fixes that exercise the new\n   tests.\n\nMoves to `satisfied` when a DEEP-mode bakeoff cycle confirms no\nregressions on the per-language collision corpus.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Whether the `disambiguation_fallback` *meta key name* is the right\n  spelling — that's a vocabulary choice the property-test PR can\n  bikeshed; the structural commitment here is \"some flag exists.\"\n- Building the upstream FQN resolver where the analyzer doesn't have\n  one (Rust receiver-type tracking, Java import resolution, etc.).\n  Those are per-analyzer work; this INV is about what to do with the\n  fallback population, not about eliminating it."  # bf11
    fields:  # bf11
      statement: "Linker-emitted edges whose target is resolved via simple-name fallback (without fully-qualified module/namespace/kind disambiguator) must carry both confidence <= 0.5 AND a disambiguation_fallback=true provenance flag, so consumers can filter the fallback population from the precision-resolved population. Applies to all edge types that participate in name resolution: extends, implements, calls, dispatches_to, references, module_exports, and any future fallback-emitting edge type."  # bf11
      root_cause: "Producers (linkers across multiple language analyzers and across edge-type families) historically each implement their own simple-name fallback when full resolution is unavailable. There is an informal 0.30/0.50 confidence convention (see notebookjournal_04062026_1634.md) and an implicit umbrella enforced via parallel test families for extends/implements across Python/JS-TS/Ruby/Kotlin/Java (see deleted INV-jodak's regression_tests field — deletion was template-cruft cleanup, not a deliberate rejection of the umbrella shape). The umbrella was never written down explicitly, so dispatches_to/calls/references-emitting linkers and the Rust implements-edge linker were never required to conform. Recurrence: ~11 done WI items + 3 new instances surfaced in hg-uat-v4.0.0 (BUG-03 / WI-zozuz; kafka Transformer collision in WI-lisov; Go interface_dispatch residual cases in WI-likab round-02 finding) + Apr 2026 Go cross-package Notifier struct collision (commit d52a1ee2b)."  # bf11
- op: discuss  # 8ab3
  at: '2026-05-04T09:58:53Z'  # 8ab3
  by: agent  # 8ab3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8ab3
  clock: 2  # 8ab3
  nonce: 8ab3  # 8ab3
  message: "Partial progress on INV-zuhub umbrella conformance.\n\nPR #3545 (commit be591c4e3) lands the first worked example of INV-zuhub conformance: the inheritance linker (packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/inheritance.py) now annotates simple-name fallback edges. When _resolve_target_symbol falls back to deterministic-by-sorted-ID disambiguation (multiple cross-file candidates, no same-file precision match), the resulting extends/implements edge carries:\n\n  confidence = 0.5\n  meta['disambiguation_fallback'] = True\n\nSingle-candidate and same-file resolutions remain at confidence=0.95 with no flag (verified by new tests test_single_candidate_resolution_keeps_high_confidence and test_same_file_resolution_keeps_high_confidence). The existing test_deterministic_fallback_when_ambiguous now asserts the conf<=0.5 + meta flag.\n\nStatus of the three concrete violations listed in the description:\n- Rust implements-edge linker (WI-zozuz): closed in PR #3543. Cross-language gating + Rust kind discipline are precision improvements that REJECT wrong matches rather than fall back. They eliminate the violation by removing the fallback path, not by annotating it.\n- JVM dispatches_to from kafka_streams_dispatch (WI-lisov Transformer<T>): unchanged. Confidence is already 0.5 but no provenance flag.\n- Go interface_dispatch residual cases (WI-likab): unchanged.\n\nWhat this PR does NOT address (and what remains for INV-zuhub satisfaction):\n1. Property tests across the listed edge-type families (extends, implements, calls, dispatches_to, references, module_exports) of the form prefers_imported / same_file_preferred / deterministic_fallback. The inheritance linker now has all three shapes for extends/implements, but the umbrella across calls/dispatches_to/references/module_exports is unbuilt.\n2. AST-walking property test that asserts every fallback-emitting linker code path sets conf<=0.5 + meta flag. This is the structural enforcement piece — without it the disambiguation_fallback discipline only holds for linkers that have been individually patched.\n3. Drift linter at pre-commit (cf. scripts/check-edge-type-drift) that fails on violations.\n4. Per-edge-type linker fixes for the kafka and Go residual cases.\n\nPattern set for downstream PRs: thread is_fallback bool back from the resolver, then at edge-creation time set confidence=0.5 + meta['disambiguation_fallback']=True when the bool is True. Two of the three concrete instances (kafka, Go) are mechanical applications of this pattern at their resolver call sites.\n\nStatus remains 'violated' since the umbrella is still unbuilt; one linker conforming to the discipline is progress but not satisfaction. The pending_validation transition condition (item 1 of acceptance criteria) requires the property tests across all listed edge types."  # 8ab3
- op: discuss  # 8736
  at: '2026-05-04T17:55:27Z'  # 8736
  by: agent  # 8736
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8736
  clock: 3  # 8736
  nonce: '8736'  # 8736
  message: "Intersection with ADR-0027 (Symbol.kind axis, draft) and ADR-0028 (Edge.evidence_type axis, draft).\n\n**ADR-0027 — kind disambiguator.** INV-zuhub's statement names *kind* as one of the three FQN dimensions a resolver should consult before falling back ('without a fully-qualified module / namespace / **kind** disambiguator'). WI-zozuz/BUG-03 exposed precisely this gap — the implements-edge linker matched on simple name without checking dst.kind=='trait'. Once ADR-0027 canonicalizes Symbol.kind (192 values / 8 clusters), resolvers gain a stable vocabulary for 'same-kind candidates only' filtering before reaching the fallback branch. The kind-disambiguation half of the WI-zozuz fix is a worked example of using kind as an FQN dimension.\n\n**ADR-0028 — resolution-status sidecar.** Bigger structural intersection. ADR-0028 explicitly promotes resolution status to a sibling Edge.is_resolved: bool (its worked example for the fold-residue discipline). INV-zuhub's meta['disambiguation_fallback']=True is structurally another resolution-status sidecar living in meta — and there's a tri-state hiding in the union:\n\n  - is_resolved=False                                  → target not in graph (external)\n  - is_resolved=True,  disambiguation_fallback=False  → resolved precisely (FQN / import-context)\n  - is_resolved=True,  disambiguation_fallback=True   → resolved by short-name fallback\n\nToday's edges only express two states (edge vs no edge). The third state — 'matched, but on weak evidence' — is what INV-zuhub is forcing into meta. The ADR-0028 work, when it lands, could either:\n\n  (a) keep disambiguation_fallback as a sibling boolean alongside is_resolved, or\n  (b) replace both with a richer resolution-quality enum, e.g.:\n      {external, fqn_resolved, import_resolved, name_fallback}\n\nEither way, today's linker-side meta writes ('meta[\"disambiguation_fallback\"]=True') are no-regrets per the fold-residue discipline ADR-0024 lays out: meta keys can be folded into whatever ADR-0028's eventual shape is, with a one-pass producer migration.\n\n**Coordination flag for the AST-walker enforcement test (acceptance item 2).** When that test is built, it must be field-agnostic in the same way ADR-0023's drift linter is — it should check 'edge carries some flag that the property test recognizes as fallback marker' rather than hardcoding the string 'disambiguation_fallback'. Otherwise the ADR-0028 fold will require simultaneous edits to the test framework. The INV-zuhub statement already anticipates this with the 'or equivalent meta key, to be standardized' hedge — but the test code must honor that hedge.\n\n**Practical sequencing.** Inheritance linker conformance (PR #3545) writes the meta key today; future linker patches (kafka, Go) follow the same pattern; the AST-walker enforcement test is built field-agnostically; ADR-0028 lands and either canonicalizes the meta key into a sibling field or folds it into a resolution-quality enum; producer migration sweeps in one pass. No work is wasted; the ordering doesn't matter as long as the AST-walker is field-agnostic."  # 8736
